  "mechanisms": [
    "Wood's naturalness attracts involuntary attention (fascination), leading to a relaxing effect and recovery of attentional resources.",
    "Multisensory perception of wood (visual, tactile, olfactory) contributes to feelings of pleasantness, warmth, and comfort.",
    "Biophilia, the human innate tendency to focus interest on life and vital processes, explains the preference for wood and its beneficial effects."
  ],
  "moderators": [
    "Degree of biophilia of people involved in the experiments (mentioned in Dematt\u00e8 et al. [8] and referenced in this paper's discussion)."
  ],
  "implementation_implications": [
    "Architects, builders, and interior designers should consider the positive psychological effects of wood when designing or renovating buildings, especially for work environments and homes.",
    "Living comfort should extend beyond physical parameters (temperature, humidity, noise, luminosity) to include psychological well-being achieved through material choices like wood."
  ],
